---
/**
 * The privacy page (Task 200 rewrite of Task 422's page). Every sentence describes what the staging code does:
 *   newsletter: src/config/site.ts (formAction posts straight to Kit);
 *   sign-in: src/islands/signin.ts (Google, or email and password with confirmation and reset emails; no magic links);
 *   PKCE code verifier keys: src/islands/supabaseclient.ts;
 *   export-my-data contents and caps: src/islands/exportload.ts (LIMITS) and exportdata.ts;
 *   delete: supabase/migrations/20261102110000_delete_my_account.sql; sessions: 20261102170000_my_sessions.sql;
 *   name-change counters: 20261102140000_update_display_name.sql; reports: 20261101120000_flags_and_submit_flag.sql;
 *   two-factor: src/islands/twofactor.ts (Supabase keeps the authenticator secret);
 *   device storage: src/scripts/{progress,resume,promo,theme}.ts, src/islands/gamestats.ts, docs/OFFLINE.md;
 *   analytics and video: astro.config.mjs (static.cloudflareinsights.com; frame-src youtube-nocookie.com).
 * Things that depend on a dashboard setting, not code, are marked as such. Not legal advice; attorney review pending.
 */
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import LegalPlaceholder from '../components/LegalPlaceholder.astro';
import LegalApproval from '../components/LegalApproval.astro';
import { isLegalPageApproved } from '../config/legalapproval';
import Prose from '../components/Prose.astro';
import { newsletter, promo, site } from '../config/site';

/** From exportload.ts LIMITS. Above any of these the download fails with one plain message and no file. */
const exportCaps = {
  attempts: 50_000,
  reviewRows: 50_000,
  examPlans: 10,
  reports: 10_000,
  sessions: 10_000,
  identities: 20,
} as const;
const format = (value: number) => value.toLocaleString('en-US');
---

<Layout title="privacy" description="What Cueory stores, what it doesn't, and how to delete or export your data." noindex={!isLegalPageApproved("privacy")}>
  <Prose>
    <h1>privacy, in plain language</h1>
    <LegalApproval page="privacy" />

    <h2>The short version</h2>
    <p>
      We keep only what we need. We don't sell your data, we place no ads of our own (a YouTube lesson video may show
      YouTube's ads after you press play), and we don't use trackers that follow you to other websites. Lessons and the public samples work without an account; practising the real questions and the
      other account features need you to sign in. If you make an account, your account page lets you download the
      account and study records we keep and delete them in one step. Data held separately by the services that work
      for us (our mailbox, Supabase's own logs and audit trail, Ko-fi) follows their own retention, described below,
      and you can ask us by email about it.
    </p>

    <h2>Who is responsible</h2>
    <p>
      {site.name} is run by <LegalPlaceholder what="legal entity name and type, the same as on the terms page" />,{' '}
      <LegalPlaceholder what="business mailing address" />. Questions about this page go to{' '}
      <a href={`mailto:${site.contactEmail}`}>{site.contactEmail}</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Right now</h2>
    <p>
      You can read lessons without an account and without giving us anything. Without an account, your study
      progress, your answers and anything you type stay in your browser; none of it is sent to us. Two things do
      happen on every visit, signed in or not, because that is how a website works: Cloudflare, which hosts the site,
      receives each page request (as any web host does), and a small Cloudflare measurement script may report that the
      page was viewed and how fast it loaded (see "Visit statistics"). Both can show which Cueory pages were opened,
      and a page address names its lesson, so they can show which lessons you read. Cloudflare receives the full
      requested URL, which may include query parameters, to serve the page; Cloudflare Web Analytics does not log
      query strings in its reports. Neither receives your answers or study progress.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you pick Light or Dark at the bottom of a page, your browser remembers that choice as{' '}
      <code>cueory-theme</code> on this device. It never reaches us.
    </p>

    <h2>Your progress on this device</h2>
    <p>
      Unless you choose to bring it into your account, your study progress is kept by your browser, on your device
      only. It is only:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>your answers to the two setup questions (which course, and your exam date), if you answer them;</li>
      <li>for each card deck, which cards you sorted into "know it" or "still learning", and which you starred;</li>
      <li>where you stopped in a study activity, with the answers you had given so far.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The saved setup answers, card piles and stars are stored as <code>cueory-progress</code> in your browser's local
      storage. This isn't an account, and it stays on the device you're using. If you're signed in, your course and
      exam date are also saved to your account.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you're signed in, your work on real questions is also saved to your account (see "If you create an account"
      below).
    </p>
    <p>
      Older per-deck stars can still be saved under <code>{'cueory-stars-<deck>'}</code> until they are moved into{' '}
      <code>cueory-progress</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Where you stopped is stored as <code>cueory-resume</code>: the activity, the page, the question you were on and
      your answers so far, so you can pick up from the same question. It never holds the answer key, but it can hold
      answers you got right. It holds nothing about your account. After 14 days it is no longer offered, and the saved
      entry is removed the next time a study page reads it, or when you clear this site's data.
    </p>
    <p>
      On <a href="/progress">your progress page</a> you can see your setup answers, piles and stars, download a copy of
      them, or forget them on this device. Clearing this site's data in your browser removes all of the above.
    </p>
    <p>
      On your first visit, a small panel about the launch list may slide down from the top of the page. Your browser
      remembers on your device that it showed you the panel, using <code>cueory-promo-seen</code>, so it stays away for{' '}
      {promo.rememberDays} days. That isn't a cookie, and it never reaches us. A separate signup pop-up may appear
      after 10 seconds of viewing; the browser saves the time under <code>cueory-signup-popup-seen</code> to keep the pop-up away
      for three hours.
    </p>
    <p>
      On <a href="/play">daily games</a>, if your browser allows storage, it automatically saves finished games on this
      device. Playing a game does not send your score to Cueory. For each result the browser can remember the puzzle
      date, whether you solved it, how many tries it took and whether you finished on a later day. Crossword results can
      record whether a solve counts toward a streak. Word path can also record how many cues you used and whether you
      found the special answer first. For sudoku, the browser saves the level, date and time taken for your first solve of
      each level each day. The games use these records to show results, times and streaks.
    </p>
    <p>
      Opening daily games or a game page checks your saved records. Anything older than 400 days is deleted. You can forget
      your stats for any single game, or forget all of your stats at once using the controls on the stats panel.
      The storage key for general daily results is <code>cueory-game-stats</code>, and sudoku’s per-level solve times
      are stored under <code>cueory-sudoku-stats</code>. Clearing browser data removes both.
    </p>
    <p>
      Daily Sudoku remembers your play preferences, such as highlighting, checking guesses, sound and timer options,
      in this browser under <code>cueory-sudoku-settings</code>. It saves those choices, not the numbers you enter
      on the puzzle board. Crossword settings are remembered under <code>cueory-crossword-settings</code>. Shared game
      settings such as sound, contrast and motion are stored under <code>cueory-game-settings</code>. Daily hard mode
      choices are remembered under <code>cueory-vialo-hard-mode</code> and <code>cueory-pillo-hard-mode</code>. Which games'
      instructions you have seen are stored under <code>cueory-game-help-seen</code>. Clearing this site's data removes
      these preferences.
    </p>
    <p>
      After you finish daily puzzles on three different days, the daily games page may suggest adding Cueory to your
      home screen. To decide when to show that suggestion, the browser keeps up to three dates on which you finished a
      puzzle and whether you dismissed the suggestion, under <code>cueory-home-screen-tip</code>. That record remains
      on this device and is not sent to us. Clearing this site's data removes it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Your browser may keep copies of the homepage, lessons you visit and site files so that you can reopen them offline.
      These copies contain the page content. Your answers and progress are stored separately. Cueory’s offline cache
      excludes daily games; daily games are never kept. That is a normal browser feature, the copies never leave your
      device, and nothing about your answers or progress is in them. Clearing this site's data in your browser removes them.
    </p>

    <h2>Features that are still being built</h2>
    <p>
      The following features are not available on the live site yet. Their browser storage is listed here before
      they launch, so you know what they will remember.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>cueory-glove-note</code>: when this feature is on, it will remember which glove notes have appeared
        during a visit in this browser tab.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>cueory-glove-note-welcome</code>: when this feature is on, it will remember the previous welcome note
        on this device, so the next visit can show a different one.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>cueory-note-sound</code>: when this feature is on, it will remember whether you switched glove-note
        sounds on or off, on this device.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>cueory-break-timer</code>: when this feature is on, it will remember your break timer's current state
        in this browser tab, for up to two hours.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>cueory-finals-survey-dismissed</code>, and <code>cueory-preview-survey</code> on the unlinked preview
        page: when the survey prompt is on, they will remember that you closed it, on this device. Nothing about an
        answer is stored or sent.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Launch-updates emails</h2>
    <p>
      The “get launch updates” email box (on the home page and in the signup pop-up) sends the email address you type
      straight to {newsletter.provider}; Cueory's own servers never receive it. No {newsletter.provider} code runs on
      our pages. If you join the list, {newsletter.provider} stores your email address and sends launch updates and
      study tips for us. You get a confirmation email first, and every email has an unsubscribe link. We will use your
      address only for {site.name} updates.
    </p>
    <p>
      That list will be separate from {site.name} accounts, so deleting an account won't take you off it. To leave it,
      use the unsubscribe link in any of those emails, or write to us.
    </p>

    <h2>Lesson videos</h2>
    <p>
      Some lessons include a video. No video loads or plays when the page first opens. If you tap Play, the YouTube
      player loads from <code>youtube-nocookie.com</code>, YouTube's reduced-tracking domain, and tries to start that
      video. From then on YouTube may set cookies, record that the video was played, and show its own ads, which we
      can't turn off.
    
    </p>

    <h2>Visit statistics</h2>
    <p>
      We measure visits with Cloudflare Web Analytics. Cloudflare adds its measurement script to our pages, and on each
      page view it reports to Cloudflare: the page address (without any query string), the page that linked to it, a
      random id for that one page load, and how the page performed in your browser (how long it took to load and the
      standard web-performance scores). Cloudflare's documentation says the script stores nothing in your browser, uses
      no cookies, and that your IP address is used to route the report to the nearest Cloudflare data centre and then
      discarded, not stored. We see totals and performance figures, not who you are. Cloudflare's documentation says
      it keeps the individual reports for 7 days and then only aggregated figures, which we can see for six months.{' '}
      <LegalPlaceholder what="confirm in the Cloudflare dashboard that Web Analytics with automatic script injection is turned on for cueory.app; if it is off, this section should say we measure nothing" />
    </p>

    <h2>Ways to support Cueory</h2>
    <p>
      Supporting {site.name} is optional, and it happens off this site. The support page links to Ko-fi; if you follow
      it, you leave {site.name}, and everything you do there is handled by Ko-fi under Ko-fi's own privacy policy, with
      the payment itself taken by PayPal or Stripe. Ko-fi's help pages say a supporter gives Ko-fi a display name and an
      email address, and may leave a message that is public on the Ko-fi page or private to the creator; Ko-fi shows us,
      as the creator, your email address and any message. Depending on the provider and payment method, PayPal or
      Stripe may also share payment-account details such as a name and email address, and Stripe may show part of the
      card-linked address. We never see your card number, and the site has no payments of its
      own. We use what Ko-fi shows us only to thank you and to keep our accounts; we don't add supporters to any mailing
      list. <LegalPlaceholder what="how long Ko-fi and PayPal/Stripe keep the supporter record on your account, and whether you keep any copy outside those dashboards" />
    </p>

    <h2>If you create an account</h2>
    <p>An account is optional. When you create one, we store only:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Your email address, so you can sign in and so we can send you account emails.</li>
      <li>
        If you sign up with a password, a scrambled form of it (a bcrypt hash) that Supabase, which runs our accounts,
        keeps so it can check your password. Nobody, including us, can read the password back out of it.
      </li>
      <li>
        A display name. Signing up with an email address asks for one (1 to 50 characters); an account made with
        Google has none until you choose one on your account page, where you can also change it. To limit how often it can be
        changed, your profile also keeps when your current hour of name changes began and how many changes you've made
        in it. Each new hour replaces the last, and both are deleted with your account and included when you download
        your data.
      </li>
      <li>If you sign in with Google, the details Google shares with us, listed below.</li>
      <li>Your sign-in sessions, with the IP address and browser of each, until you sign out or the session expires.</li>
      <li>Your answers to a few setup questions, such as your exam date.</li>
      <li>
        Your study progress: what you've studied, your answers, how long you spent on each question, which ones you
        marked know it or still learning, and when to review next. We use it to show your progress and to find which
        lessons and questions need improving.
      </li>
      <li>
        Reports you send with the "Flag or comment" button: the page you were on, the question if there was one, the
        reason you picked and your comment, if you wrote one. We keep a report after we've dealt with it, until your
        account is deleted. Your account data download will include them. Never put a real patient's details in a
        comment. If you did so by accident, email us straight away with the page and roughly when you sent it, and we
        will review the report and remove it where we can;{' '}
        <LegalPlaceholder what="define who removes such a report, how it is identified, how removal is verified and the turnaround, before any deadline is promised; a single-report delete function with its own tests is a later pull request" />.
        This covers reports sent through the button while signed in, which go into our database. If you are signed
        out, the button opens your email program instead, and anything you email us (a flag, a question, a privacy
        request) sits in our mailbox, not in the database: deleting your account doesn't delete emails, so ask us and we
        will delete them;{' '}
        <LegalPlaceholder what="confirm the mailbox provider for hello@cueory.app, how long mail is kept there, and your deletion practice" />.
      </li>
      <li>When your account was created and when you last signed in, which Supabase records for every account.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Account emails are the ones you ask for by acting: a link to confirm your email address when you sign up, and a
      link to reset your password when you ask for one. We never send marketing to account addresses. Today Supabase
      sends those emails; we plan to send them through Resend later, and this page will say so when that changes.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you choose Google sign-in, you are sent to Google to finish signing in. Google gives Supabase profile
      information such as your name, a link to your profile photo, your Google account id, your email address and
      whether Google has checked it. Depending on the profile and provider response, the identity record can contain
      additional provider profile fields. Supabase keeps the identity details with your account and may refresh them
      on later sign-ins. {site.name} does not display your Google profile photo, but your account data export includes
      the photo URL and the other provider identity details Supabase returns.{' '}
      <LegalPlaceholder what="Christian: verify which optional Google profile fields this project's Supabase configuration stores and exports using a safe test account, then list those categories before approving the privacy page" />
    </p>
    <p>
      Each time you sign in, Supabase keeps a sign-in session with your IP address and the kind of browser and device
      you used. A session lasts until you sign out or until it expires; a session can also end when you change your
      password or when Supabase's limits on session length or inactivity are reached, and Supabase removes expired
      session records about a day later. <LegalPlaceholder what="confirm the session limits under Authentication > Sessions in the Supabase dashboard (time-box, inactivity timeout, single session) and state them here, or say 'no limit is set'" />{' '}
      Supabase's own service logs, which include IP addresses, are kept for a short period set by Supabase's plan;{' '}
      <LegalPlaceholder what="confirm the log retention period for the project's plan on supabase.com/pricing and in the dashboard (the Free plan's documented retention is 1 day)" />.
      Supabase Auth also logs account security events such as sign-ups, sign-ins and password resets. Supabase stores
      those audit logs separately from your account. The project may optionally write audit entries to a database table,{' '}
      <code>auth.audit_log_entries</code>. We have not confirmed whether that setting is enabled for Cueory. Deleting
      your account may not remove those separate audit records;{' '}
      <LegalPlaceholder what="Christian: check Supabase Authentication > Audit Logs for the optional Write audit logs to the database setting, confirm audit-log retention and whether deletion removes those records, and update this paragraph before approving the privacy page" />.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you turn on two-factor sign-in at <a href="/account/two-factor">/account/two-factor</a>, Supabase keeps your
      authenticator app's secret key with your account so it can check your codes. Removing two-factor there deletes it.
    </p>
    <p>
      When you're signed in, the browser keeps your sign-in session under <code>cueory-auth</code>. It includes your
      account details, such as your email address. That is different from the study progress kept on your device.
      While you sign in, the browser also keeps a PKCE code verifier, a random secret that proves the sign-in that
      started in this browser is the one that finishes here, under <code>cueory-auth-code-verifier</code>,{' '}
      <code>{'cueory-auth-flow-<id>-code-verifier'}</code> and <code>cueory-auth-flows-code-verifier</code>. Signing out
      removes them.
    </p>
    <p>
      Once accounts open, you will be offered to bring this browser's guest progress into your account. The import
      saves your course and exam date as an exam plan first, then any card piles that match published questions as
      review schedules; today no cards are matched to published questions, so only the course and exam date come in,
      and your card piles and stars stay on this device. The guest copy is left on the device, so a failed import can
      be retried. The browser uses <code>{'cueory-guest-imported:<id>'}</code> to remember that it has already imported
      for that account, or that you chose not to. Here, <code>{'<id>'}</code> stands for that account's identifier,
      not a public name.
    </p>

    <h2>Signing out is not the same as deleting</h2>
    <p>
      Signing out ends your sign-in session in this browser and removes <code>cueory-auth</code> and the sign-in
      codes. It does not remove the study progress, setup answers or saved place kept in this browser
      (<code>cueory-progress</code>, <code>cueory-resume</code> and the other keys above), so on a shared or public
      computer the next person could see them. Until a one-tap "forget this device" action exists, use "forget" on{' '}
      <a href="/progress">your progress page</a> and clear this site's data in the browser before you leave a shared
      device. Deleting your account is different: it removes your account and its data on our side, as described below.
    </p>

    <h2>Your rights: download or delete</h2>
    <p>
      On the <code>/account</code> page you can download your data or request deletion without emailing us. Deleting
      an account may require you to sign in again if your sign-in is not recent enough, so we can check that it is you.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong class="key-term">Download your account data.</strong> The account-held part includes your profile
        (display name, when it was created, and the name-change counters), your email address, your attempts, exam
        plans, review schedules, study events, reports and sign-in sessions, and your Supabase sign-in record: account
        creation and last sign-in dates, sign-in methods and the details each provider shared.
        The same download also includes data saved only in the browser you are using: your course and exam setup,
        card piles and stars, plus saved daily-game results if you turned on game stats. Those browser-only records are
        read from this device to create the file, not uploaded to Cueory just to make the download, and may differ
        on another device. The download is available as JSON or CSV files.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="key-term">Change your display name</strong>, a few times an hour.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="key-term">Delete your account.</strong> After any required fresh sign-in, confirming deletion
        removes your account and all study data linked to it in one step, all or nothing, and signs you out of this
        browser. It can't be undone. Cueory then tries to clear the progress and settings it saved in this browser; if
        the browser blocks that, the page tells you so and asks you to clear this site's data yourself. The offline
        copies of pages hold only public files and are not removed by deleting an account; clearing the site's data
        removes them. Supabase's service logs, described above, expire on their own schedule.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The download is all or nothing, and it has limits: if your account holds more than {format(exportCaps.attempts)}{' '}
      attempts, {format(exportCaps.reviewRows)} review schedules, {format(exportCaps.examPlans)} exam plans,{' '}
      {format(exportCaps.reports)} reports, {format(exportCaps.sessions)} sign-in sessions or{' '}
      {format(exportCaps.identities)} sign-in methods, the download fails with a plain message and no file. Study events
      have no limit. If that happens, or if you can't sign in, email us;{' '}
      <LegalPlaceholder what="confirm how you would supply an export by hand (for example a CSV from the Supabase dashboard) and how fast; until then this page promises only that you will answer" />.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can also email us to ask what we hold about you, to correct it, or to delete it. Before we share or delete
      anything we will confirm that you control the account, for example by asking you to reply from or sign in to the
      registered account;{' '}
      <LegalPlaceholder what="define the identity-verification and fulfilment steps for emailed requests before launch, including what happens when someone has lost access to the registered mailbox and how an export is delivered securely; nothing specific is promised until then" />. We never sell personal information, we don't share
      it for advertising. The site does check your practice answers automatically and schedules your reviews from them;
      it makes no automated decision about you with legal or similarly significant effects.{' '}
      <LegalPlaceholder what="with a lawyer: confirm this automated-processing sentence against any law that applies" /> Wherever you live, these are the same tools
      for everyone; if the law where you live gives you further rights, write to us and we will honour them.
    </p>

    <h2>Copyright notices</h2>
    <p>
      If you send us a copyright notice or a counter-notice (see the <a href="/copyright">copyright page</a>), it
      contains your contact details, and a counter-notice is passed in full to the person who sent the original notice,
      because that is how the process works. We keep notices and counter-notices with our records;{' '}
      <LegalPlaceholder what="with a lawyer: whether the formal notice-and-counter-notice process applies to Cueory at all, and how long notices are kept" />.
    </p>

    <h2>Children</h2>
    <p>
      {site.name} is for nursing students. Nobody under 13 may create an account or send us personal information, and
      our <a href="/terms">terms</a> set a higher minimum age for accounts. If you believe a child has given us
      personal information, email us so we can investigate the report without exposing anyone else's records.{' '}
      <LegalPlaceholder what="Christian and counsel: document how to verify a parent or guardian, locate a child's records when they cannot sign in, and securely carry out any legally required removal before promising a deletion process or deadline" />
    </p>

    <h2>Keeping it safe</h2>
    <p>
      Every page is served over HTTPS. Private account and study rows are protected by row-level security, so students
      can read only their own private records. Real published-question answer keys are checked through
      permission-controlled database functions rather than included with the public question data. Public demonstration
      questions are different: they include local-checking data and sample keys in the browser, and those sample keys
      are not confidential. Administrative records remain access-controlled. Today we keep no backup copies of account
      data beyond what Supabase itself keeps;
      if we start making our own, this page will say where they are kept and for how long. If personal data were ever
      exposed, we would tell the people affected and the authorities as the law requires.
    </p>

    <h2>Who handles data for us</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Cloudflare hosts the website, serves every page, and counts visits.</li>
      <li>{newsletter.provider} stores newsletter signups and sends launch-update emails.</li>
      <li>
        Supabase stores account and study data for people who create an account, their sign-in sessions, password
        hashes and two-factor secrets, and sends account emails. Google handles Google sign-in if you choose it.
      </li>
      <li>Resend is planned for sending account emails and is not in use yet.</li>
      <li>
        YouTube plays lesson videos. Nothing loads from YouTube until you tap play, and it may set cookies once a video
        plays.
      </li>
      <li>Ko-fi, with PayPal or Stripe, handles support payments on its own site, if you choose to go there.</li>
      <li>
        Our email provider holds whatever you email to {site.contactEmail}: questions, signed-out flags, privacy
        requests;{' '}
        <LegalPlaceholder what="name the mailbox provider for hello@cueory.app (the domain's mail records were reported to point at iCloud Mail; confirm), confirm the mailbox is read, who can see emailed reports, and how long mail is kept and how it is deleted on request" />.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      These services keep data on their own servers, which may be in a different country from you. We don't share
      your data with anyone else except when the law requires it.
    </p>

    <h2>Changes to this page</h2>
    <p>
      When we change what we store or who handles it, we update this page first and change the effective date at the
      top. For a change that matters to account holders, we also tell you by email or with a notice on the site.
    </p>

    <h2>Questions</h2>
    <p>
      Email <a href={`mailto:${site.contactEmail}`}>{site.contactEmail}</a>. You can ask what we hold about you or ask us
      to delete it.
    </p>
  </Prose>
</Layout>
